      text: `<p></p> <p>Consider the database for a web application being created to serve lower income patients in a new medical network, DontCare, being proposed by the recently elected leaders of a certain country. The primary keys for the two tables, Patient and Doctor, are pid and did, respectively.<br><br> \\$\\$  \\begin{array}{|c|l|l|c|c|l|c|} \\hline \\textbf{pid} &amp; \\textbf{lastname} &amp; \\textbf{firstname} &amp; \\textbf{age} &amp; \\textbf{spouse} &amp; \\textbf{symptoms} &amp; \\textbf{doc} \\\\ \\hline 1 &amp; Doe &amp; Jane &amp; 30 &amp; \\textit{null} &amp; none &amp; 1002 \\\\ 2 &amp; Smith &amp; Laura &amp; 25 &amp; 3 &amp; chills &amp; 1000 \\\\ 3 &amp; Smith &amp; David &amp; 24 &amp; 2 &amp; fever &amp; 1001 \\\\ 4 &amp; Lee &amp; Richard &amp; 40 &amp; \\textit{null} &amp; none &amp; 1000 \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}  \\$\\$ <br> \\$\\$  \\begin{array}{|c|l|l|c|} \\hline \\textbf{did} &amp; \\textbf{name} &amp; \\textbf{specialty} &amp; \\textbf{certyear} \\\\ \\hline 1000 &amp; Frankenstein &amp; surgery &amp; 1950 \\\\ 1001 &amp; Jekyll &amp; dermatology &amp; 1994 \\\\ 1002 &amp; Dre &amp; audiology &amp; 2000 \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}  \\$\\$ <br><br>Now consider the following SQL transactions that users might wish to run against this table: </p> <p>\\[<br>\\begin{array}{l}<br>\\textbf{\\underline{Transaction T1:}} \\[4pt]</p> <p>\\textbf{BEGIN TRANSACTION;} \\</p> <p>\\qquad \\textbf{INSERT INTO} \\ \\text{Patient} \\ \\textbf{VALUES} \\<br>\\qquad\\qquad (5,\\ \\text{'Deere'},\\ \\text{'John'},\\ 52,\\ \\text{null},\\ \\text{'aches'},\\ 1000); \\</p> <p>\\qquad \\textbf{INSERT INTO} \\ \\text{Patient} \\ \\textbf{VALUES} \\<br>\\qquad\\qquad (6,\\ \\text{'Doe'},\\ \\text{'John'},\\ 33,\\ 1,\\ \\text{'pains'},\\ 1000); \\</p> <p>\\qquad \\textbf{UPDATE} \\ \\text{Patient} \\<br>\\qquad\\qquad \\textbf{SET} \\ \\text{spouse} = 6 \\ \\textbf{WHERE} \\ \\text{pid} = 1; \\</p> <p>\\textbf{END TRANSACTION;}<br>\\end{array}<br>\\]<br>\\begin{array}{l}<br>\\textbf{\\underline{Transaction T2:}} \\[8pt]</p> <p>\\textbf{BEGIN TRANSACTION} \\</p> <p>\\qquad \\textbf{SELECT} \\ \\text{COUNT}(*) \\<br>\\qquad \\textbf{FROM} \\ \\text{Doctor d, Patient p} \\<br>\\qquad \\textbf{WHERE} \\ d.did = p.doc \\<br>\\qquad\\qquad \\textbf{AND} \\ d.name = \\text{'Frankenstein'}; \\</p> <p>\\textbf{END TRANSACTION;}<br>\\end{array}<br>Suppose that you were to run transactions  \\( T_1 \\) and  \\( T_2 \\) concurrently in different windows on your laptop, and further suppose that you wisely configured your database system's consistency level to be SERIALIZABLE. Select all of the possible numerical outcomes from  \\( T_2 \\)'s query (depending on how it interleaves with  \\( T_1 \\)). </p> <p></p>`,
